            0.846,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Parallel and Intersecting Lines"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.393,
            0.086,
            0.605,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.464,
                0.192,
                0.537,
                0.207
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.26"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.216,
            0.846,
            0.254
          ],
          "content": "**Solution:** $\\angle 6$ is $1 3 5 ^ { \\circ }$ , so $\\angle 2$ is also $1 3 5 ^ { \\circ } ,$ , because it is the corresponding angle of $\\angle 6$ and the lines l and m are parallel."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.253,
            0.845,
            0.29
          ],
          "content": "∠8 is $1 3 5 ^ { \\circ }$ , because it is the vertically opposite angle of $\\angle 6 . \\angle 4$ is $1 3 5 ^ { \\circ }$ because it is the corresponding angle of $\\angle 8 .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.29,
            0.845,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "$\\angle 2$ is $1 3 5 ^ { \\circ }$ because is the vertically opposite angle of $\\angle 4.\\; \\mathsf{S}0, \\angle 2, \\angle 4,$ $\\angle 6 ,$ and $\\angle 8$ are all $1 3 5 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.326,
            0.847,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": "∠5 and $\\angle 6$ are a linear pair, together they measure $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ }$ . If $\\angle 6$ is $1 3 5 ^ { \\circ }$ , then"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.363,
            0.376,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "\\angle 5 = 180 - 135 = 45^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_15_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.381,
            0.757,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "We can similarly find out that $\\angle 1 , \\angle 3 ,$ , and $\\angle 7$ measure $4 5 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.409,
            0.846,
            0.45
          ],
          "content": "？ **Example 2:** In Fig. 5.27, lines l and m are intersected by the transversal t. If $\\angle a$ is $1 2 0 ^ { \\circ }$ and $\\angle f$ is $7 0 ^ { \\circ }$ , are lines l and m parallel to each other?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.4,
            0.455,
            0.602,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.486,
                0.592,
                0.558,
                0.606
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.27"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.611,
            0.846,
            0.668
          ],
          "content": "**Solution:** $\\angle a$ is $1 2 0 ^ { \\circ }$ , so $\\angle b$ is $6 0 ^ { \\circ }$ because $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ form a linear pair. $\\angle b$ is a corresponding angle of $\\angle f .$ If l and m are parallel, $\\angle b$ should be equal to $\\angle f ,$ however, they are not equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.667,
            0.847,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, lines l and m are not parallel to each other as the corresponding angles formed by the transversal t are not equal to each other."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.713,
            0.846,
            0.753
          ],
          "content": "**Example 3:** In Fig. 5.28, parallel lines l and m are intersected by the transversal t. If $\\angle 3$ is $5 0 ^ { \\circ }$ , what is the measure of $\\angle 6 7$"
        },
        {
